{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0aa2aa6b4f35a3800169bb382cf47c8191943d5dda6f0b23d9", "created": 1773877292, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"IMRT External Beam Radiation Community (Radiation Beams, Dose, Photons/Electrons)\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on external radiation therapy concepts, with Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT) as the most connected entity. IMRT is positioned as an advanced external beam radiation therapy technique within the broader domain of radiation therapy and is described as enabling high, targeted tumor dosing while better sparing nearby organs at risk (e.g., external genitalia, hip joints). Supporting entities describe how radiation therapy uses directed radiation beams (including photons and electrons) and how treatment plans specify the prescribed radiation dose, typically delivered in fractions. A related, adjacent concept (brachytherapy) appears as another radiation modality emphasizing limiting dose to surrounding normal tissue.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"IMRT is the central technique and is framed as advanced external-beam radiation therapy\",\"explanation\":\"IMRT is described as an advanced form of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) and explicitly linked as a technique within radiation therapy more broadly. It is also directly categorized as a type of external radiation therapy, placing it within the external (non-implant) radiation delivery family. [Data: Entities (4766, 6428); Relationships (10364, 14671)]\\n\\nThe available information also notes practical availability constraints: IMRT “may or may not be offered at a given facility” and may require referral depending on local availability and care pathways, indicating that access and service configuration can affect whether a patient receives IMRT versus other techniques. [Data: Relationships (10364)]\"},{